Concrete Canopy

Ingredients: BEBOP + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Bongos
Creative Catalyst: its founding artists were botanists who sonified the electrical signals of rainforest plants.

It sounds like a rusted fire escape dropped into a humid jungle greenhouse; rapid-fire, angular horn lines collide with the bone-dry, hand-slapped wood of skin bongos and the heavy, unyielding low-end thud of raw concrete architecture. The music moves with an erratic, breathless syncopating pulse, weaving complex bebop changes through raw, aggressive sonic textures and acoustic percussion that sounds both intensely organic and punishingly heavy.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 210–240 BPM; breakneck, irregular swing fueled by acoustic hand percussion and relentless forward momentum.
- Harmonic Palette: Altered dominants, sharp tritones, and minor-ninth suspensions that evoke industrial decay intertwined with aggressive tropical overgrowth.
- Production Signatures: Direct-in-board horn distortion, extreme close-miking of bongo shells to capture every fibrous wood-slap, and massive brutalist room reverb mixed with near-zero high-end polish.
- Dynamics: Abrupt transitions from whisper-quiet, delicate botanical micro-voltages to deafening, wall-of-sound full ensemble explosions.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the damp, concrete-reinforced basements of a botanical research institute in late 1974, this scene was forged by disillusioned phytologists who began routing bio-electric sap-flow data from carnivorous orchids through custom modular oscillators. Rejecting the sterile ivory tower of academic jazz, these scientists turned underground squats into brutalist jungle-dens, wearing grease-stained lab coats over thrifted zoot suits. Their rituals involved setting up dense arrays of bongo drums around towering concrete pillars, playing lightning-fast horn lines that dueled with the erratic, clicking voltage spikes of starving jungle flora.

Instruments Used
Bongos, unmuted hollow-body electric archtop guitars, battered alto saxophones playing blisteringly fast chromatic runs, custom low-frequency brass-plate resonators, and raw ribbon microphones.

For Fans Of: Eric Dolphy, The Art Ensemble of Chicago, Sun Ra, Throbbing Gristle

